{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q012_not_true_family_specific_pair","answer":"Not in the evolutionary sense. In SCOPe 2.08, d1s9za_ (1s9z A:, protein "Amyloidogenic design-1", sunid 103785) and d1hqja_ (1hqj A:, protein "Unnamed design-1", sunid 100906) are classified in the same family. That family is k.40.1.1 "Designed trimeric coiled-coil" (sunid 100905). It sits in superfamily k.40.1 (sunid 100904), fold k.40 (sunid 100903), class k "Designed proteins" (sunid 58788). They are different Protein-level nodes, and both have the species "Synthetic".

The class is "Designed proteins". Both are artificial, de novo designed sequences, not natural proteins. Sharing a SCOPe family normally implies homology, and the API returns homology_implied_by_level=true with no warnings, heterogeneity or inconsistencies for either domain. But for a designed-protein class that implication is only a classification convention. It means the two are grouped by shared structure and design concept, a trimeric coiled-coil. It does not show common natural ancestry. I would not call these "true" homologs, and the family label does not make this a family-specific homologous pair. Similar designs are better described as analogous or convergent, or as related by design.

The practical answer is that SCOPe 2.08 places them in the same family, superfamily and fold, which would normally imply homology. Given the designed (synthetic) class, read that as structural/design relatedness. Do not transfer function (for example the amyloidogenic behavior, or the metal-binding/ligand context from the comments: 1s9z complexed with Na and Zn, 1hqj with Pb, succinate and sulfate) from one design to the other. Also do not infer an evolutionary relationship, and do not infer sequence-level similarity beyond the shared coiled-coil fold. This classification is not evidence of shared function or a natural evolutionary lineage.","evidence_used":["mcp__scope__compare_scop_domains","mcp__scope__get_scop_domains","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1s9za_","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1s9za_/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1s9za_/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1s9za_/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1hqja_","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1hqja_/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1hqja_/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1hqja_/homology"]}]}
